// `std:str` — the string method surface VL's core does not have.
//
// The core's whole string vocabulary is SIX operations — `slice`, `indexOf`,
// `includes`, `charCodeAt`, `fromCodePoint`, `toString` — plus `+`, `==`,
// `.length`, `s[i]` and `for cp in s`. Everything a program actually does to
// text is missing: you cannot split a string in VL, cannot trim one, cannot
// replace inside one, cannot ask whether one ends with another.
// `docs/guide/strings-design.md` §Methods calls this "the largest user-facing
// gap in the whole area", and this module is its **step 1**.
//
// WHY THIS IS A LIBRARY AND NOT COMPILER WORK. Every operation here is
// expressible over the primitives the core already has, so none of it needs the
// emitter, a new intrinsic, or a representation change — and that is exactly
// why the design doc sequences it FIRST. The rep change that follows (UTF-8
// bytes behind a slice header, §Storage/§Header) has to be validated against
// something, and six methods' worth of fixtures is a thin instrument against an
// emitter whose known failure mode is a silently missing ladder arm. The
// fixtures under `tests/cases/std/str-*.vl` are that instrument; they are
// written against the SURFACE (`.length`, `s[i]`, `slice`) and not the storage,
// so they survive the swap unchanged and fail loudly if it goes wrong.
//
// SELF-FIRST, SO EVERY EXPORT IS A METHOD. `self` is VL's UFCS switch (B14): a
// free function whose first parameter is named `self` is reachable as
// `receiver.name(rest)`. `"a,b".split(",")` and `split("a,b", ",")` are the
// same call, and a string RECEIVER resolves it exactly like a struct one
// (verified — `typecheck.vl`'s string-method arm falls through to `ufcsCallTy`
// after the four builtins). The one export that breaks the pattern is `join`,
// whose receiver is a `string[]`; that is not an inconsistency but the shape of
// the operation, and `std:fmt` already spelled it that way.
//
// ── EVERY BUILDER HERE FILLS A CODE-POINT BUFFER. READ THIS BEFORE EDITING. ──
//
// The obvious way to build a string in VL — `let s = ""` then `s = s + piece`
// in a loop — is **O(n²) on this compiler**, and that is a measured fact rather
// than a caution. `DECISIONS.md` (B7b) records a string-accumulation fusion
// that turns exactly that shape into an amortized buffer fill; the fixtures for
// it still sit in `tests/cases/strings/accum-*.vl`. But that optimization was
// implemented in `compiler/toWasm.ts` (PR #168) and **deleted with the TS core**
// (PR #466) — it was never ported to the native emitter, and
// `grep -rn "accumulat" compiler/*.vl` finds no recognizer. The accum fixtures
// still pass because they only assert the RESULT, which per-append concat also
// produces; they are blind to the cost class they were written to pin.
//
// Measured on this head, `s = s + "abcdefgh"` in a loop, whole-run wall clock:
//
//     n = 20000  →   313 ms
//     n = 40000  →  1502 ms   (×4.8)
//     n = 80000  →  7614 ms   (×5.1)
//
// Doubling the input quintuples the time. That is quadratic, and it is why no
// function in this file accumulates into a string. The repo's established
// answer is a single code-point buffer materialized once — `compiler/format.vl`'s
// `joinOut`, `fmt_util.vl`'s `collapseWs` and `joinLinesRange` all carry the
// scar tissue, the last of them noting that the accumulator it replaced "blew
// the compiler's NON-FREEING null-GC heap" on a large file. `pushRange` /
// `pushStr` / `fromCodePoints` below are that idiom, factored once.
//
// The rule for anyone extending this module: **an `i32[]` grows, a `string`
// does not.** `buf.push(c)` is an amortized append; `s + c` copies the whole
// left operand. Two `+`s to splice a result (`replace`) are fine — that is
// O(n) once, not O(n) per element.
//
// And the second half of the rule, which is about the UNIT rather than the
// cost: **what goes into that buffer is a CODE POINT, and it gets there by
// `for cp in s`, never by `s[i]`.** `fromCodePoints` reads its argument as code
// points — its name is its contract — and `for cp in s` yields code points both
// today and after the UTF-8 swap, so the two move together. `s[i]` does not:
// §API makes it a BYTE, and a byte pushed into a code-point buffer comes back
// out as a different character (`fromCodePoints([195, 169])` is `"Ã©"`, not
// `"é"` — run it). `pushStr` is where that discipline is enforced for the whole
// file; `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` is the audit that found it.
//
// ── ASCII IS IN THE NAME WHERE ASCII IS THE BEHAVIOUR ──
//
// `toUpperAscii` / `toLowerAscii` are spelled that way DELIBERATELY, and the
// design doc rules it: an unqualified `toUpper` that silently leaves `é` alone
// is the quiet-lie class VL exists to reject — it works on every string the
// author tests and is wrong on the first one a user types. Full case mapping
// needs Unicode tables (and is not even a per-code-point map: `ß` upper-cases
// to two characters, and Turkish dotless-i makes it LOCALE-dependent), so it
// belongs to a future `std:unicode` alongside `normalize`, `graphemes` and
// `reverse`. The name here promises exactly what it does.
//
// `trim` is ASCII-only and is NOT named for it, which this comment used to call
// a deliberate asymmetry. **CORRECTION — the precedent it claimed is not
// there.** It said the six-character set is "the same six C's `isspace` takes
// and Go's `strings.TrimSpace` starts from". "Starts from" is the tell: Go's
// six are a FAST PATH, and `TrimSpace` falls through to `TrimFunc(s,
// unicode.IsSpace)` the moment it meets a byte >= 0x80 — so Go trims NBSP.
// So do Rust (`char::is_whitespace`, the Unicode White_Space property), Python
// (`str.strip()`), and JS (`String.prototype.trim`, WhiteSpace + LineTerminator).
// All four of the languages this module's rulings defer to strip U+00A0; VL
// implemented Go's optimization and stopped, which is not the same as
// implementing Go.
//
// The behaviour is UNCHANGED here — an audit is not the place to move a shipped
// answer — but it is now an OPEN RULING rather than a settled asymmetry, and
// the argument on each side (including the good one for ASCII-only: a lexer
// wants exactly these six, and White_Space is 25 code points that need no
// tables) is written up in `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §R3. Note the
// question is rep-INDEPENDENT: byte-scanning for ASCII whitespace stays exactly
// correct after the UTF-8 swap, because no byte of a multi-byte sequence is
// ever < 0x80. It surfaces here only because the audit had to check it.
//
// ── THE DEGENERATE INPUTS ARE RULED, NOT LEFT TO FALL OUT ──
//
// Every export below documents its own edges and every one is pinned by a
// fixture. The rulings all resolve the same way: **follow the unanimous
// precedent**, because `DECISIONS.md` already put the core's string methods in
// the JS camp ("`indexOf("")` returns 0") and a std module that disagreed with
// its own core would be the seam this language is written to avoid. Where JS,
// Python, Rust and Go agree — and on the empty-needle question all four do —
// that agreement is the answer. The two consequences worth stating up front:
//
//   - An empty separator SPLITS INTO CHARACTERS, so `"".split("")` is `[]`
//     (nothing to split) while `"".split(",")` is `[""]` (one empty field).
//     Those look inconsistent side by side and are not: a separator that
//     appears zero times always yields exactly one field.
//   - An empty `from` MATCHES AT EVERY BOUNDARY, so `"abc".replaceAll("", "-")`
//     is `"-a-b-c-"` — `to` before the first character, between each pair, and
//     after the last. Go's own documentation spells this out ("if old is empty,
//     it matches at the beginning of the string and after each UTF-8 sequence"),
//     and Rust/Python/JS produce the identical string.
//
// ── THE ONE LAW WORTH REMEMBERING ──
//
//     s.replaceAll(from, to) == s.split(from).join(to)      for a non-empty `from`
//
// It holds for every input, including the ones people get wrong (a separator at
// the very start, at the very end, adjacent, or overlapping), and it is pinned
// as a fixture rather than left as a claim. It is why `split` and `replaceAll`
// share `findFrom` and agree about overlap: matches are consumed
// non-overlapping, left to right, so `"aaa".replaceAll("aa", "b")` is `"ba"`
// and not `"bb"` or `"b"`. For an EMPTY `from` the law does not hold and cannot
// — the boundary rule above puts `to` at both ends, and `split("")` does not —
// which is stated here so nobody "fixes" one side into agreement with the other.
//
// ── WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO ──
//
// NO REGEX, NO PATTERN ARGUMENT. Every `from`/`sep`/`prefix` here is a literal
// string. `std:regex` is its own module in the design doc's core-vs-std table,
// wants a compiled pattern type, and would put a second coordinate system next
// to these byte offsets; a half-regex hidden behind a `split` overload is worse
// than none.
//
// NO `splitN` / `splitOnce` / `rsplit`, NO `trimPrefix`/`trimSuffix`, NO
// `replaceFrom(i)`. Each is a real operation with real callers elsewhere, and
// none has a caller in this tree yet — `docs/internals/std-design.md` D1's
// admission principle is that nothing lands without one. They are all purely
// additive over `findFrom`/`lastIndexOf`, so the day a consumer appears they
// cost a few lines. A name that does not exist yet is the only one still free
// to be right.
//
// NO `contains`, NO `indexOf`, NO `slice`. Those are core builtins on `string`
// already; re-exporting them would create a SECOND name for one operation and,
// because VL has no namespace import, a program that imported both would take a
// hard duplicate-binding parse error. `lastIndexOf` is here because the core has
// no counterpart at all.
//
// NO CODE-POINT-COUNTING ANYWHERE, AND NO UNIT CONVERSION EITHER. `.length`
// and every index in this file is whatever the core's index unit is — today a
// code point, after the rep swap a UTF-8 byte — and nothing here converts
// between the two or asks which one it is. Offsets are produced by `indexOf`/
// `length` and consumed by `slice`/`matchAt` in the same unit, so they cancel.
// The one place a unit is NAMED is the builder buffer, which is code points by
// `fromCodePoints`'s contract and is filled by iteration for exactly that
// reason (see the buffer rule above).
//
// The two places the unit is still LOAD-BEARING, and therefore still open:
// `padStart`/`padEnd`'s `len` argument, which is a width rather than an offset
// and so has nothing to cancel against; and the EMPTY-needle rule in `split`/
// `replaceAll`, which asks what a "boundary" is. Both are written up with
// options and a recommendation in `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md`.
//
// ── RELATIONSHIP TO `std:fmt` ──
//
// `std:fmt` used to own `split`, `join`, `repeat` and `padLeft`. It no longer
// implements any of them: it re-exports the first three from here and its
// `padLeft` is a one-line call into `padStart`, so there is exactly one
// implementation of each in the tree. That is not tidiness — it is a bug fix.
// VL has no namespace import, so two modules exporting `split` means
// `import { toStr } from "std:fmt"` and `import { split } from "std:str"` in one
// file is fine but `import { split } from "std:fmt"` beside it is a HARD PARSE
// ERROR, and which module a caller reached for would have silently decided
// whether their join was O(n) or O(n²). `std:fmt` keeps the name for its
// existing importers; `std:str` keeps the code.

// ── the shared cores ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Whether `needle` occurs at index `at`. The one comparison primitive: every
// search in this file (`findFrom`, `lastIndexOf`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`)
// is a policy about WHICH `at` to try, and none of them re-spells the compare.
//
// It reads element by element rather than `self.slice(at, at + needle.length)
// == needle` because the slice ALLOCATES — an allocation per candidate position
// turns every search in the module into garbage pressure proportional to the
// haystack. An out-of-range `at` (negative, or a needle that would run off the
// end) is `false`, not a trap, so callers may hand it an unchecked offset;
// `endsWith` relies on that for a suffix longer than the string.
function matchAt(self: string, needle: string, at: i32): boolean {
  if at < 0 { return false }
  if at + needle.length > self.length { return false }
  let i = 0
  while i < needle.length {
    if self[i + at] != needle[i] { return false }
    i = i + 1
  }
  true
}

// The index of the first occurrence of `needle` at or after `from`, or -1.
//
// The core's `indexOf` takes no start offset (checked: `typecheck.vl` types it
// at exactly one argument), which is the reason this exists — a scan that
// resumes is what `split` and `replaceAll` are made of.
//
// An EMPTY needle is found immediately at `from`, matching the core's
// `indexOf("") == 0`; `from` past the end yields -1 for a non-empty needle and
// `from` itself for an empty one, which is what makes the empty case terminate
// in callers that advance by `needle.length` — they must, and do, special-case
// it instead.
function findFrom(self: string, needle: string, from: i32): i32 {
  let i = from
  if i < 0 { i = 0 }
  const last = self.length - needle.length
  while i <= last {
    if matchAt(self, needle, i) { return i }
    i = i + 1
  }
  -1
}

// Append every code point of `s` to `buf`. The single copy primitive of this
// module — see the header: `buf` GROWS (amortized push), a string would be
// COPIED WHOLE per append.
//
// IT ITERATES RATHER THAN INDEXES, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT STYLE.
// `buf` is drained by `fromCodePoints`, whose contract is fixed by its name:
// each `i32` is a CODE POINT. `for cp in s` yields code points too, and
// `docs/guide/strings-design.md` §Codepoints keeps it yielding code points
// after the UTF-8 swap — so the producer and the consumer are a matched pair
// whose meanings move together, i.e. not at all. `buf.push(s[i])` was the
// SAME VALUE today and a different one after: `s[i]` becomes a BYTE, and
// pushing the two bytes of `é` into a code-point buffer re-encodes them as
// two separate characters. `fromCodePoints([195, 169])` is `"Ã©"` on this very
// build — measured, not predicted — so every builder in this file (`join`,
// `replaceAll`, `repeat`, `padFill`, `mapAsciiCase`) would have shipped
// mojibake the day the representation changed. See
// `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §Builders.
function pushStr(buf: i32[], s: string) {
  for cp in s { buf.push(cp) }
}

// Append `s[lo..hi)` to `buf`. `lo` is clamped up to 0 and `hi` down to the
// end, so a caller may pass an empty or inverted range.
//
// The clamp on `lo` is EXPLICIT and not delegated to `slice`, because VL's
// `slice` reads a negative index as JS does — from the END (`"abc".slice(-1, 3)`
// is `"c"`, checked) — which is the opposite of the "clamp to the start" this
// helper promises. `hi` needs no such care: an over-long `hi` clamps the same
// way in both.
function pushRange(buf: i32[], s: string, lo: i32, hi: i32) {
  let start = lo
  if start < 0 { start = 0 }
  pushStr(buf, s.slice(start, hi))
}

// The six ASCII whitespace characters — space, tab, newline, vertical tab, form
// feed, carriage return.
//
// Whether the set SHOULD stop at ASCII is an open owner ruling: the header
// records the correction (all four of JS, Python, Rust and Go strip Unicode
// spaces such as NBSP; Go's six are a fast path, not its definition) and
// `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §R3 has the options. It is
// rep-INDEPENDENT — a byte test for a value below 0x80 stays exactly correct
// after the UTF-8 swap, because no byte of a multi-byte sequence is ever that
// low — so nothing about the migration forces the question either way.
function isSpace(c: i32): boolean {
  c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\v' || c == '\f' || c == '\r'
}

// The index of the first non-whitespace character, or `self.length` when there
// is none (an all-blank string, which includes the empty one).
function spanStart(self: string): i32 {
  let i = 0
  while i < self.length {
    if !isSpace(self[i]) { return i }
    i = i + 1
  }
  self.length
}

// One PAST the index of the last non-whitespace character, or 0 when there is
// none. Paired with `spanStart` these bound the trimmed span as a half-open
// `[a, b)` — and on an all-blank string they cross (`a == length`, `b == 0`),
// which the callers handle explicitly rather than leaning on `slice`'s clamp.
function spanEnd(self: string): i32 {
  let i = self.length
  while i > 0 {
    if !isSpace(self[i - 1]) { return i }
    i = i - 1
  }
  0
}

// ── searching ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Whether `self` begins with `prefix`. An empty prefix is always present
// (every string begins with nothing), a prefix longer than `self` never is.
export function startsWith(self: string, prefix: string): boolean {
  matchAt(self, prefix, 0)
}

// Whether `self` ends with `suffix`. An empty suffix is always present; a
// suffix longer than `self` computes a NEGATIVE offset, which `matchAt` refuses
// rather than trapping — that is the reason for its `at < 0` guard.
export function endsWith(self: string, suffix: string): boolean {
  matchAt(self, suffix, self.length - suffix.length)
}

// The index of the LAST occurrence of `needle`, or -1 when absent — the missing
// counterpart to the core's `indexOf`, and it keeps `indexOf`'s -1 convention
// rather than inventing a `i32 | null` the core would disagree with.
//
// Scanning DOWN from the last position a match could start at means the first
// hit is the answer, so no candidate is examined twice. An empty needle
// returns `self.length` (it occurs at the boundary after the last character,
// which is the last of the `length + 1` places it occurs) — the mirror of
// `indexOf("") == 0`, and what JS, Python, Rust and Go all report.
// Occurrences may overlap here, unlike in `split`/`replaceAll`, because a
// single position is being reported rather than a sequence consumed:
// `"aaa".lastIndexOf("aa")` is 1.
export function lastIndexOf(self: string, needle: string): i32 {
  let i = self.length - needle.length
  while i >= 0 {
    if matchAt(self, needle, i) { return i }
    i = i - 1
  }
  -1
}

// ── splitting and joining ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The pieces of `self` between each occurrence of `sep`, left to right.
//
// The result ALWAYS has one more element than `sep` has occurrences, which is
// the single rule every edge below follows from:
//
//   - `"a,b,c".split(",")` → `["a", "b", "c"]`
//   - a separator at the very START gives a leading empty piece:
//     `",a".split(",")` → `["", "a"]`
//   - at the very END, a trailing one: `"a,".split(",")` → `["a", ""]`
//   - ADJACENT separators give an empty piece between them:
//     `"a,,b".split(",")` → `["a", "", "b"]`
//   - a separator that never occurs gives the whole string as one piece:
//     `"abc".split(",")` → `["abc"]`
//   - an EMPTY input therefore gives one empty piece: `"".split(",")` → `[""]`
//   - matches do not OVERLAP, they are consumed left to right:
//     `"aaa".split("aa")` → `["", "a"]`
//
// An EMPTY separator is the one case outside that rule: it splits into single
// CODE POINTS, so `"abc".split("")` → `["a", "b", "c"]` and — this is the edge
// people trip on — `"".split("")` → `[]`, an EMPTY list rather than `[""]`.
// "Code points" is the word that matters after the UTF-8 swap: an empty
// separator is the only input in this file where "every boundary" has two
// possible readings, and the loop below iterates rather than indexes so the
// answer stays the one it gives today — which is also Go's ("if sep is empty,
// Split splits after each UTF-8 sequence"). `"é".split("")` is one piece, not
// two half-characters. The alternative reading is an OPEN RULING;
// `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §R2 has it.
// The two empty-input answers differ because the questions differ: splitting
// "" on "," finds no separator and hands back the one field it was given,
// while splitting "" into its characters finds none. Every one of JS, Python
// (via `list(s)`), Rust `.chars()` and Go `strings.Split` agrees; so did the
// `std:fmt` implementation this replaces.
//
// The pieces are `slice`s of `self`, which is O(1) and allocation-free once the
// rep change lands (§Header) — splitting a 1 MB file into 10k lines will
// allocate 10k headers and copy zero bytes. It copies today.
export function split(self: string, sep: string): string[] {
  const out: string[] = []
  if sep.length == 0 {
    for cp in self { out.push(fromCodePoint(cp)) }
    return out
  }
  let start = 0
  let at = findFrom(self, sep, 0)
  while at >= 0 {
    out.push(self.slice(start, at))
    start = at + sep.length
    at = findFrom(self, sep, start)
  }
  out.push(self.slice(start, self.length))
  out
}

// The elements of `self` concatenated with `sep` between each adjacent pair —
// `split`'s inverse, and the receiver is a `string[]` rather than a string
// because that is what the operation is ON.
//
// An empty list joins to `""` and a one-element list to that element, both
// without touching `sep` — so `[""].join(",")` is `""` and `["", ""].join("-")`
// is `"-"`, one separator for the one gap. There is no trailing separator,
// ever.
//
// One buffer, one `fromCodePoints`: the `out = out + parts[i]` spelling of this
// function is the exact loop that went quadratic and blew the compiler's heap
// (`compiler/fmt_util.vl:216`). Joining 40,000 eight-character pieces, whole-run
// wall clock, same 359,999-character result: **28 ms buffered against 12,475 ms
// accumulated** — the two spellings are not a tuning difference, they are two
// complexity classes, and this is the A/B behind the header's rule. The single-
// and zero-element early returns are not micro-optimization either: they are
// what makes joining a one-piece list allocation-free, which is the common
// result of splitting on a separator that was not there.
export function join(self: string[], sep: string): string {
  if self.length == 0 { return "" }
  if self.length == 1 { return self[0] }
  const buf: i32[] = []
  let i = 0
  while i < self.length {
    if i > 0 { pushStr(buf, sep) }
    pushStr(buf, self[i])
    i = i + 1
  }
  fromCodePoints(buf)
}

// ── replacing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `self` with the FIRST occurrence of `from` replaced by `to`. When `from` does
// not occur, `self` comes back unchanged and untouched — the same object, no
// allocation.
//
// An EMPTY `from` occurs at index 0 (the core's own `indexOf("") == 0` rule),
// so `"abc".replace("", "-")` is `"-abc"`: `to` is prepended. JS, Python and
// Rust all produce that exact string.
//
// Two `+`s and no buffer, deliberately: this splices at ONE point, so it is a
// pair of O(n) copies rather than the O(n)-per-element accumulator the header
// warns about. `replaceAll` cannot say the same and does not try.
export function replace(self: string, from: string, to: string): string {
  const at = findFrom(self, from, 0)
  if at < 0 { return self }
  self.slice(0, at) + to + self.slice(at + from.length, self.length)
}

// `self` with EVERY occurrence of `from` replaced by `to`.
//
// Matches are consumed left to right and never overlap, because the scan
// resumes PAST the match it just took: `"aaa".replaceAll("aa", "b")` is `"ba"`
// — the first two characters are consumed, the third is left. This is the same
// consumption rule `split` uses, which is what makes the law in the header
// (`replaceAll(f, t) == split(f).join(t)` for a non-empty `f`) hold on exactly
// these awkward inputs. A `to` that CONTAINS `from` is not rescanned, so
// `"a".replaceAll("a", "aa")` terminates at `"aa"`.
//
// An EMPTY `from` matches at every CODE-POINT boundary — before the first
// character, between each pair, and after the last — so `"abc".replaceAll("",
// "-")` is `"-a-b-c-"` and `"".replaceAll("", "-")` is `"-"`. That is JS,
// Python, Rust and Go verbatim (Go documents it as "up to k+1 replacements for
// a k-rune string" — note *rune*), and it is the one input where the split/join
// law does not hold. The loop iterates code points rather than indexing, so
// after the UTF-8 swap it still puts `to` between CHARACTERS and not between
// the bytes of one: a byte-boundary reading would make `"é".replaceAll("", "-")`
// the invalid `"-\xC3-\xA9-"`. Same open ruling as `split("")`
// (`docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §R2).
//
// A `from` that never occurs returns `self` unchanged with nothing allocated —
// checked BEFORE the buffer is built, because "no match" is the common case for
// a caller sanitizing input that is usually already clean.
export function replaceAll(self: string, from: string, to: string): string {
  const buf: i32[] = []
  if from.length == 0 {
    for cp in self {
      pushStr(buf, to)
      buf.push(cp)
    }
    pushStr(buf, to)
    return fromCodePoints(buf)
  }
  let at = findFrom(self, from, 0)
  if at < 0 { return self }
  let start = 0
  while at >= 0 {
    pushRange(buf, self, start, at)
    pushStr(buf, to)
    start = at + from.length
    at = findFrom(self, from, start)
  }
  pushRange(buf, self, start, self.length)
  fromCodePoints(buf)
}

// ── trimming ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `self` without leading ASCII whitespace. An all-blank string trims to `""`;
// a string with nothing to remove comes back unchanged and unallocated.
export function trimStart(self: string): string {
  const a = spanStart(self)
  if a == 0 { return self }
  if a == self.length { return "" }
  return self.slice(a, self.length)
}

// `self` without trailing ASCII whitespace. All-blank trims to `""`.
export function trimEnd(self: string): string {
  const b = spanEnd(self)
  if b == self.length { return self }
  if b == 0 { return "" }
  return self.slice(0, b)
}

// `self` without leading or trailing ASCII whitespace — interior whitespace is
// UNTOUCHED (`" a b ".trim()` is `"a b"`, not `"ab"`).
//
// This is not `trimStart(trimEnd(self))`: that spelling allocates an
// intermediate string for the common case where both ends need work. One pass
// for each bound, one slice. The all-blank case is checked FIRST because the
// two bounds cross there (`spanStart` reports `length`, `spanEnd` reports 0),
// and leaning on `slice`'s start >= end clamp to paper over it would be relying
// on a clamp to express an intent.
export function trim(self: string): string {
  const a = spanStart(self)
  if a == self.length { return "" }
  const b = spanEnd(self)
  if a == 0 && b == self.length { return self }
  return self.slice(a, b)
}

// ── building ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `self` concatenated `n` times. `n <= 0` yields `""` — a negative count is
// treated as zero rather than trapping, because "repeat this negative-one
// times" is a computed count that came out empty, not a bug in the program.
// (JS throws a RangeError here; Rust and Go's `strings.Repeat` panic. VL's
// error-handling design reserves traps for bugs, and the useful reading of a
// count that fell below zero is an empty result.)
export function repeat(self: string, n: i32): string {
  if n <= 0 { return "" }
  if n == 1 { return self }
  if self.length == 0 { return "" }
  const buf: i32[] = []
  let i = 0
  while i < n {
    pushStr(buf, self)
    i = i + 1
  }
  fromCodePoints(buf)
}

// Exactly `n` index units of `pad`, tiled and truncated wherever the tiling
// lands — `padFill(3, "ab")` is `"aba"`. `n <= 0` or an empty `pad` yields
// `""`, which is what makes both padders return their input untouched instead
// of special-casing the two degenerate arguments twice.
//
// Truncating mid-tile rather than refusing a multi-character pad is what JS
// does, and it is the behaviour that makes the result EXACTLY `len` long, which
// is the entire point of padding (a column that is sometimes one unit wider is
// not a column).
//
// "INDEX UNIT", NOT "CHARACTER", AND THE TWO ARE THE SAME THING TODAY. `n`
// arrives from `padStart`/`padEnd` as `len - self.length`, so whatever
// `.length` counts is what this tiles — code points today, UTF-8 bytes after
// `docs/guide/strings-design.md` §API lands. Spelling it `repeat` + `slice`
// rather than `buf.push(pad[i % pad.length])` keeps it in lockstep with
// `.length` through that change instead of pushing bytes into a code-point
// buffer (see `pushStr`), and it is the same string today: `repeat("ab", 2)`
// sliced to 3 is `"aba"`. **Whether `len` SHOULD be a byte count at all is an
// open owner ruling** — `docs/internals/str-byte-semantics.md` §R1 lays out the
// options and recommends code points, which every surveyed language including
// byte-indexed Go uses for padding. If that ruling lands, this function and the
// `self.length` in both padders move together; nothing else in the file does.
function padFill(n: i32, pad: string): string {
  if n <= 0 { return "" }
  if pad.length == 0 { return "" }
  if n <= pad.length { return pad.slice(0, n) }
  // `return` is not optional here: a bare member CALL in tail position is
  // `emitProgram: unsupported member-call statement` at emit time (checked;
  // `docs/guide/strings-design.md` §Methods records the same shape).
  return repeat(pad, (n + pad.length - 1) / pad.length).slice(0, n)
}

// `self` padded on the LEFT with `pad` until it is `len` long.
//
// Returns `self` untouched when it is already `len` or longer — padding never
// TRUNCATES, so `"hello".padStart(3, "0")` is `"hello"` and not `"hel"`. That
// is JS's rule and the only safe one: a padder that shortened its input would
// silently destroy data in exactly the logging and table-formatting code that
// calls it. An empty `pad` is likewise a no-op rather than an infinite loop.
export function padStart(self: string, len: i32, pad: string): string {
  const fill = padFill(len - self.length, pad)
  if fill.length == 0 { return self }
  fill + self
}

// `self` padded on the RIGHT with `pad` until it is `len` long. Same rules as
// `padStart`: never truncates, empty `pad` is a no-op, the result is exactly
// `len` long whenever padding happened at all.
export function padEnd(self: string, len: i32, pad: string): string {
  const fill = padFill(len - self.length, pad)
  if fill.length == 0 { return self }
  self + fill
}

// ── ASCII case ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The distance from `c` to its ASCII case counterpart, or 0 when it has none —
// the one place the alphabet is named, so `mapAsciiCase` carries no direction
// logic and neither exported wrapper carries any at all.
//
// Every code point outside `a`–`z` / `A`–`Z` maps to 0 and therefore passes
// through byte-identical. That includes every non-ASCII character, which is the
// promise the `Ascii` suffix makes.
function caseShift(c: i32, up: boolean): i32 {
  if up {
    if c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' { return -32 }
    return 0
  }
  if c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z' { return 32 }
  0
}

// The one case walk, shared by both exported directions.
//
// It SCANS before it builds: a string with nothing to change — already-upper
// text, a number, a punctuation run, anything non-ASCII — is returned as it
// arrived, with no buffer and no allocation. That matters because the common
// call is a normalizing comparison (`a.toLowerAscii() == b.toLowerAscii()`)
// where one side is usually already lower-case.
function mapAsciiCase(self: string, up: boolean): string {
  let hit = false
  for cp in self {
    if caseShift(cp, up) != 0 {
      hit = true
      break
    }
  }
  if !hit { return self }
  const buf: i32[] = []
  for cp in self { buf.push(cp + caseShift(cp, up)) }
  fromCodePoints(buf)
}

// `self` with every ASCII lower-case letter upper-cased. NOTHING else changes —
// `"é"`, `"ß"` and every other non-ASCII character pass through untouched, and
// the name says so. Full Unicode case mapping is a future `std:unicode`; see
// the header for why it cannot be this function under a shorter name.
export function toUpperAscii(self: string): string { mapAsciiCase(self, true) }

// `self` with every ASCII upper-case letter lower-cased. Same promise, same
// limit, same reason for the name.
export function toLowerAscii(self: string): string { mapAsciiCase(self, false) }
